---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import Panel from '../../components/Panel.astro';
import ev from '../../data/mealie-evidence.json';
import '../../styles/mealie.css';

// Every number on this page comes from src/data/mealie-evidence.json: the
// GitHub API, the running container and its daily Trivy scan, all read 10-31.
const up = ev.upstream;
const tls = ev.tls_fix;
const img = ev.image;
const lib = ev.library;
const k = (n: number) => `${(Math.floor(n / 100) / 10).toFixed(1)}k`;
const md = (d: string) => d.slice(5);
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Mealie fork"
  path="/projects/mealie/"
  description={`My fork of Mealie, the self-hosted recipe manager: a fix merged upstream in ${up.released_in}, a TLS-verification bug found and fixed in my own scraper code, and a runtime image slimmed from ${img.python_packages_before} to ${img.python_packages_now} Python packages.`}
  lede={`A fork of Mealie, the self-hosted recipe manager (${k(up.stars)} stars upstream). I moved my recipes off a commercial app whose search only read titles and that had no nutrition data. I build the images, run them at home, and send generic fixes upstream: one is merged and credited in ${up.released_in}.`}
  tags={['Python', 'Vue', 'Docker', 'CI/CD', 'Supply chain', 'Open source']}
  kicker="Open source · fork"
  accent="fork"
  slug="mealie"
  stats={[
    { n: up.released_in, l: 'my fix merged and credited upstream' },
    { n: `${tls.after_pass}/${tls.after_total}`, l: 'TLS tests pass after fixing my own scraper' },
    { n: `${img.python_packages_before} → ${img.python_packages_now}`, l: 'Python packages in my image, no dev tools left in it' },
    { n: `${lib.multi_category}`, l: `of my ${lib.recipes} recipes in 2+ categories, protected by the fix` },
  ]}
  demonstrates="Python and Vue across the stack, upstream contribution through maintainer review, finding and fixing a security bug in my own code with tests, container supply-chain hygiene (slim runtime image, daily Trivy, acceptance by digest with rollback), and CI/CD on a fork."
  links={[
    { label: `Merged upstream: PR #${up.pr}`, href: 'https://github.com/mealie-recipes/mealie/pull/7881' },
  ]}
>
  <SectionHead label="Upstream" title={`The bug behind PR #${up.pr}`} />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> recipes with servings but no yield published
    a yield of 0 in their schema.org data, and 0 is the denominator for
    anything per serving.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mealie emits schema.org JSON-LD for every recipe, which is how search
    engines, scrapers and nutrition trackers read it. The yield string was built
    from the yield <em>quantity</em> and <em>text</em>, and a validator turns an
    unset quantity into zero. So a servings-only recipe emitted a bare
    <code>"0.0"</code>: not missing, zero.
  </p>

  <Panel label="mealie · schema.org output" status="real logic">
    <div class="jsonld-demo" data-jsonld>
      <div class="map-controls">
        <span class="ctl-label">build</span>
        <div class="ml-seg" role="group" aria-label="Build">
          <button class="ml-opt" type="button" data-fix="before" aria-pressed="true">before #{up.pr}</button>
          <button class="ml-opt" type="button" data-fix="after" aria-pressed="false">after #{up.pr}</button>
        </div>
        <span class="ctl-label">yield</span>
        <div class="ml-seg" role="group" aria-label="Yield type">
          <button class="ml-opt" type="button" data-ytype="servings" aria-pressed="true">servings only</button>
          <button class="ml-opt" type="button" data-ytype="text" aria-pressed="false">quantity + text</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="map-controls" data-servings-wrap>
        <label class="ctl-label" for="servings">count</label>
        <input id="servings" type="range" data-servings min="1" max="12" step="1" value="4" />
        <output class="ctl-out" data-servings-out>4</output>
      </div>

      <pre class="code-out" data-json aria-label="emitted schema.org JSON-LD"></pre>
      <div class="consumer" data-consumer aria-live="polite"></div>

      <p class="sample-note">
        With a quantity-and-text yield it was only cosmetic
        (<code>"8.0 slices"</code>), which is why it survived: the case that
        broke the math only hit servings-only recipes, in metadata nobody reads
        by eye. The fix covers both, with parametrized tests over every yield
        shape. A maintainer tightened the fallback in review so a text-only yield
        never gets servings put in front of it, merged it on
        {' '}{md(up.merged)}, and it shipped in {up.released_in} the next day with
        a first-contributor credit.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <SectionHead label="Security" title="A finding in my own code" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> the recipe scraper my fork carries fetched
    pages with TLS certificate checks turned off. I found it, fixed it, and
    proved it with tests before trusting the image.
  </p>
  <article class="find ml-find" data-card>
    <p class="label">fork scraper · fixed {md(tls.date)}</p>
    <h3>TLS verification was off in {tls.places} places</h3>
    <p class="find-tldr">Anyone on the network path could have fed the recipe parser whatever they liked.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>found</dt>
      <dd>
        A review of my fork's scraper: page fetch, short-link resolve and an
        embed lookup all passed <code>verify=False</code>. Upstream Mealie does
        not have this; it came in with code my fork added.
      </dd>
      <dt>before</dt>
      <dd>Test sites with an expired, self-signed, wrong-host and untrusted-root certificate were all fetched.</dd>
      <dt>fix</dt>
      <dd>
        One transport verifies by default (certifi). The only exception is an
        exact host list set by the admin, validated at startup, and every skip
        is logged. A failed check stops the fetch with a clear message instead
        of retrying without verification.
      </dd>
      <dt>proof</dt>
      <dd>
        {tls.tests_added} new test files. On the deployed image: all 4 bad
        certificates refused, a normal site and a plain-HTTP site fetched:
        {' '}{tls.after_pass}/{tls.after_total} pass, accepted by image digest.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </article>

  <SectionHead label="Supply chain" title="A smaller image, accepted by digest" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> the image I run carries only what the app
    needs, is scanned daily, and is never trusted until a script checks the
    exact digest that is running.
  </p>
  <ul class="ml-facts">
    <li>
      <strong>Slimmed:</strong> {img.python_packages_before} Python packages
      became {img.python_packages_now}, and the running image has no pip, pytest,
      ruff or mypy in it (checked {md(ev.captured)}). High-severity CVEs went
      {' '}{img.high_before_slim} → {img.high_after_slim} from that alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Patched:</strong> a dependency bump on 10-29 cleared every
      critical CVE in the image that had a fixed version available, verified by
      the daily scan.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Accepted, not assumed:</strong> each new image goes through an
      acceptance script: digest match, health, login, the public page, and the
      change's own tests. The previous image stays tagged for rollback.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Built in the open:</strong> every push to the fork builds and
      publishes a public image to GitHub's registry. The one running now was
      built {md(img.built)} from commit {img.running_rev}.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <SectionHead label="Data integrity" title="The one that could quietly empty my cookbooks" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> the update call replaces a recipe's whole
    category list, so sending only the tapped category silently removes all
    the others. {lib.multi_category} of my {lib.recipes} recipes sit in more
    than one category.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tapping the pill on a card files the recipe into a cookbook. The obvious
    implementation sends the category you tapped. Tap a category below and
    watch the shelf.
  </p>

  <Panel label="mealie · category filing" status="sample data">
    <div class="wipe-demo" data-wipe>
      <div class="map-controls">
        <span class="ctl-label">a toggle sends</span>
        <div class="ml-seg" role="group" aria-label="What a toggle sends">
          <button class="ml-opt" type="button" data-mode="naive" aria-pressed="true">just the tapped category</button>
          <button class="ml-opt" type="button" data-mode="additive" aria-pressed="false">read, merge, send all</button>
        </div>
        <span class="sep"></span>
        <button class="ml-reset" type="button" data-reset>reset the recipe</button>
      </div>

      <div class="wipe-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="rcard">
            <div class="rcard-photo">
              <span class="rcard-cat"><i class="dot"></i><span data-pill>Dinner</span><i class="caret">▾</i></span>
              <span class="rcard-kcal">412 kcal</span>
            </div>
            <div class="rcard-body">
              <h3 class="rcard-title">Cheeseburger Baked Potato Boats</h3>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="menu">
            <p class="menu-head">file into…</p>
            <ul class="menu-list" data-menu></ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="possess-label">
            cookbooks this recipe is actually in
            <span class="wipe-count" data-wipe-count></span>
          </p>
          <ul class="shelf" data-shelf></ul>
          <pre class="code-out" data-wire aria-label="the PATCH request this toggle sends"></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="wipe-damage" data-damage></p>

      <p class="sample-note">
        My real cookbook names, minus one I am not putting on a portfolio. The
        fix is four lines: read the current list, merge, send all of it. The
        naive version works on any recipe in one folder, and most hand-written
        tests start with a recipe in one folder. Checked on the wire before I
        trusted it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <SectionHead label="The redesign" title="A shape problem, not a colour problem" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> the theme set colours but never component
    defaults, so every card kept stock Material shapes under any palette.
  </p>
  <p>
    Adding defaults in that one file restyled every page: recipes, search,
    cookbooks, meal planner, settings. Small diff, easy to rebase, because
    upstream rarely touches it. Calories on the card needed a separate backend
    change that put nutrition in the list response.
  </p>

  <Panel label="mealie · recipe card" status="recreated in css">
    <div class="ui-demo" data-ui>
      <div class="map-controls">
        <span class="ctl-label">recipe card</span>
        <div class="ml-seg" role="group" aria-label="Recipe card">
          <button class="ml-opt" type="button" data-ui-mode="stock" aria-pressed="true">palette only</button>
          <button class="ml-opt" type="button" data-ui-mode="editorial" aria-pressed="false">defaults + rebuild</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ui-stage stock" data-ui-stage></div>
      <p class="sample-note" data-ui-note></p>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <SectionHead label="Release engineering" title="The CI that went green and built nothing" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> upstream's publish job only runs on
    upstream's repo, so on a fork it is skipped and the run still shows green.
  </p>
  <ul class="ml-facts">
    <li>
      <strong>The silent skip:</strong>
      <code>if: github.repository == 'mealie-recipes/mealie'</code> is false on a
      fork. The job is skipped, the run reports success, and no image is built.
      Repointed at the fork.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The rest:</strong> a paid build service I have no token for,
      swapped for stock buildx with layer caching; publishing to my own
      namespace; the image name lowercased because the registry rejects
      capitals.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The trap:</strong> the GitHub CLI in a fork defaults to the
      parent repo. Unless every command names the fork, you read upstream's CI
      and conclude your build never ran.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <script is:inline src="/js/mealie-demo.js" defer></script>
  <script is:inline src="/js/wipe-demo.js" defer></script>
</ProjectLayout>
